entries.body:
`startGame(canvas, wasmUrl, options)` turns a Nish module written over nish/host/game into a running browser game.

What it does:
- loads the module asynchronously with streaming instantiation;
- presents its framebuffer through nish/page/frame (WebGPU, then WebGL 2, then Canvas 2D, with fallback when a device is lost), and rebinds its listeners on the fresh canvas;
- maps the keyboard and pointer/touch zones into the input ring, letting modified browser shortcuts through and releasing held keys on blur and when the page is hidden;
- plays the sound queue through WebAudio, unlocking it on an activating gesture and retrying until it runs;
- runs step and draw at the display's own rate;
- stops cleanly and calls onError when the game throws.

examples/game-starter is a small complete game. A reader copies it anywhere and builds it against the installed nish package. The new "Writing a browser game" guide page walks through it.

runtime-wasm.c now defines nish_arena_keep as the native runtime's refusal, so string code reached from wasm links without a stub.
